  "Question": "If \\(x\\) is such that \\(\\dfrac1x\\lt2\\) and \\(\\dfrac1x\\gt-3,\\) then:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac13\\lt x\\lt\\dfrac12\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac12\\lt x\\lt3\\)",
    "C": "\\(x\\gt\\dfrac12\\)",
    "D": "\\(x\\gt\\dfrac12\\) or \\(-\\dfrac13\\lt x\\lt0\\)",
    "E": "\\(x\\gt\\dfrac12\\) or \\(x\\lt-\\dfrac13\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The reciprocal requires \\(x\\ne0\\). Before multiplying an inequality by \\(x\\), separate its possible signs, because a negative multiplier reverses the inequality.\n\nIf \\(x>0\\), then \\(1/x>0>-3\\), so the lower bound is automatic. The remaining inequality gives\n\\[\n\\frac1x<2\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad1<2x\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x>\\frac12.\n\\]\nIf \\(x<0\\), then \\(1/x<0<2\\), so the upper bound is automatic. Multiplying the lower bound by the negative number \\(x\\) reverses its direction:\n\\[\n\\frac1x>-3\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad1<-3x\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x<-\\frac13.\n\\]\nThese cases cover every nonzero real \\(x\\). The endpoints give equality in one original bound and are excluded. Thus the solution is \\(\\boxed{x>\\frac12\\text{ or }x<-\\frac13}\\), choice E.",
